      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Honestly the easiest solution to the majority of unskilled rippers would be to force a cache clear on exiting the game. It won't solve all of it but it will prevent the majority of ripping being done by users with the most surface level knowledge of what they are doing.\n\nOr at the very least take advantage of the kernel level access EAC provides and force constant cache erasures on anyone found to posses known ripping software?",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-05-20T15:22:13.766Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Players are currently able to extract other users' original content from the game at a lossless quality. \nThere are several user-made solutions, but they bloat file sizes and can be easily reverse-engineered; They're only band-aids to the real issue, where ripping from the game is so effortless it has been automated. \nRipping can never be fully prevented, but it can be made harder than what it currently is.\nSome methods (from an uneducated person) that might be viable:\n- Store assets in packages that don't follow unity standards \n- not storing all data in the same place, send data in pieces\n- encryption that's decrypted in computer memory\nAgain, nothing is foolproof and ripping *will* happen, but methods like these would slow down and hopefully deter some perpetrators. VRChat continues to make significant improvements to the game and they inspire people to create, but an increasing number of these creators spend more and more time in private worlds or have quit the game altogether, having been disheartened from their private works being stolen and sold.\nYou can report if you're lucky and come across your content in the sea of millions, or just have to learn to live with the fact for now. But it shouldn't be this way. One of the main aspects of the game is creating and sharing your creations, so this aspect should be protected.\nThank you for making VRChat, many of us have found a new life through it!",
